#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "0acc185c-cc26-523f-981a-3e024f0693a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3589" :question/problem "Is the function $y=g(x)$ below left-continuous at $x=0$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/365a2e8f5b5d5e0b8872647cb38cea17.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $g(x)$, is left-continuous at $x=0$, we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nIndeed, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} g(x) = -2 = g(0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "e9a8cbce-3bed-567b-9e0e-e1382465a681" :question/math-academy-id "e-3590" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=1$, then what is $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 3^+}} \\left(f(x)-x^2\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3^+} f(x)=f(3)=1\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → 3^{+})(f(x) - x^{2}) = 1 - 3^{2} =-8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "1ec6e675-6052-5cec-8b29-766084245f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-3591" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/412190f4780d9243ce6ed015c2f67bf6.png)\n\n1. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n2. The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n3. The function is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0$, since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n$\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to0^-}g(x) = 2 \\neq -2 = g(0)$\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0$, since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to0^+}g(x) = -2 = g(0)$\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0$, it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd419f7-d872-57d3-bfee-9f241feafe62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251729} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c3c0aa-0999-5613-bfcb-97e3ffc6d1c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251730} {:answer/id #uuid "b972fc2f-51d3-5e72-bf75-32cd8beadf13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251731} {:answer/id #uuid "84ce691c-0b18-5fe2-b430-28c925561502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251732} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7361fa-3bd2-5829-b0da-40975f839aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a7361fa-3bd2-5829-b0da-40975f839aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c249743-73da-5b32-a686-f0c5727d5e79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251728}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74f21211-cc3c-54cf-8784-8fd096842237" :question/math-academy-id "q-104285" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9$ and $f(-9)=4,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}f(x)=f(-9)=4.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}=(4-(-9))^{2}=169.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be8de47e-c8bf-51ff-892f-072125cf6a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251736} {:answer/id #uuid "28a07236-8fc2-52a3-a627-eec7c5aa3925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251737} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd73b91-058d-52cc-8867-1a5761d5c95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251738} {:answer/id #uuid "5a67c79c-749c-5e54-ae82-c55a357465b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251739} {:answer/id #uuid "6dba1311-cb9b-503f-b9cf-793ab31e4a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be8de47e-c8bf-51ff-892f-072125cf6a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251736} :answer-field/id #uuid "184bcb9a-6276-5c4f-afca-2bd5006e8692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251735}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc9779cc-da7d-58d1-bd14-f30104ff71bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-104357" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/30df82b4c47905fd8277067f9207a5e970a96d8ec386879d2872b5f5c3d8dd3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "751c5fff-2657-50cf-a2c1-c63e32c21b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251743} {:answer/id #uuid "1fdf86a3-cae6-5d09-a08c-2f3ae45fb149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251744} {:answer/id #uuid "93659e81-69d5-57eb-a2d0-3828ed1c7279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251745} {:answer/id #uuid "ba500de3-7e87-5ca0-8f56-e95d8df3a675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251746} {:answer/id #uuid "f0412890-d59e-5222-b528-ddd45e1cc8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fdf86a3-cae6-5d09-a08c-2f3ae45fb149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251744} :answer-field/id #uuid "167adaf6-18c7-5864-aded-fc8a7f549f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251742}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b25ac4a7-2d94-5cb4-b0e8-f671f8e0d4b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104358" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/7c95fb061d6b860655e934f2691d03e53f7bb77a617200f523f1695ee49083fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false.  The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdea37-b24e-5d1b-85c2-287592cf22dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acdea37-b24e-5d1b-85c2-287592cf22dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251750} :answer-field/id #uuid "01aacdc2-7e87-599a-bde9-7f9c7ea4a42b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ef9c30f-4a7e-5e86-9fd3-8b9955c73729" :question/math-academy-id "q-339585" :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10$ and $g(10)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)}=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)=g(10)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)-{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-\\operatorname{log}⁡10}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f982f4b-0a43-5957-a096-7ed259b0a574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251753} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7d4618-52eb-519a-b7d5-479a6da2c3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf7d4618-52eb-519a-b7d5-479a6da2c3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251754} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c5832d-07e2-595d-afe1-ac6be838fa8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251752}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c3e571a-4724-5af9-bcc0-f9ca20412934" :question/math-academy-id "q-339592" :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5$ and $g(5)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x}=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)=g(5)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)-\\text{ }{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}{x}^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-{5}^{2}}{4(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-25}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d48e51-e895-517b-9a09-d87b29aaba2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251757} {:answer/id #uuid "347740f4-fe94-500c-870d-738b6323e770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "347740f4-fe94-500c-870d-738b6323e770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251758} :answer-field/id #uuid "23495c8d-030c-57a0-9a16-a93380bb783c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251756}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "224c62c3-d8c3-5a74-a48f-f28670c74d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-339597" :question/problem "If $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10$ and $h(10)=15,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10))=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)=h(10)=15.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10)) & ={lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}x\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(10) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 15-5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =150-75 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea9ad21e-441b-55ea-8219-051fd4298f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea9ad21e-441b-55ea-8219-051fd4298f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251761} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f95881-1911-5674-bb10-e4c875e39d4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8960d1d-052a-50ff-88f3-364a4c6c5c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8960d1d-052a-50ff-88f3-364a4c6c5c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251763} :answer-field/id #uuid "13031d09-e88f-5c61-bf6d-c5d64d7aedcb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251762}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7444952-e689-5026-9ea6-0702c532dc85" :question/math-academy-id "q-341375" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/f223c6b96b4ce7e522d1529145ae90d7d68927e3234aa891a7be30a345355a9e.png)\n\nFrom **left to right**, the function $y=f(x)$ above is left-continuous but is not continuous at\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}} $\\,$ and  \n$\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point. $\\,\\times $\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2=f(1).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(3).\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=1$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "724420fc-6fa0-5034-b16a-c970cec521e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "724420fc-6fa0-5034-b16a-c970cec521e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251766} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e772ba-52d9-52b3-a48e-7ddf83af5b76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c31086e-5f9d-542a-8ac5-193b4a7832dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c31086e-5f9d-542a-8ac5-193b4a7832dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251768} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed81b16-5ce8-5842-85b3-f808e335dd19" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3dbf1cc-f8fe-5c56-b884-f7d15af77f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-341379" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/f4f20d86a96988d657aeac166b6228cc938523e0ba6100908725aa83b6c88781.png)\n\nFrom **left to right**, the function $y=f(x)$ above is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}} $\\,$ and  \n$\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-3$, $x=-1,$ and $x=1.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(-1).\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(1).\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n$x=-3$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488bc865-d437-563b-95d6-2139e3e2a8b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251771} {:answer/id #uuid "e454d8c9-2c04-513f-b191-f37b766975ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e454d8c9-2c04-513f-b191-f37b766975ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251772} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c78726-f95b-5d89-8616-a46065fdb064" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e581ab1-1c0e-5e8a-a3db-36c8d21f3746" :question/math-academy-id "q-341576" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/ef59e4b5c39770c5bcade3475a23d2631d2db10fea4fd7c925be092bbf819e61.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=1=f(-1).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(3).\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95989bbf-ce3e-5609-a6a5-2c014e7cdf61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066930} {:answer/id #uuid "9838f976-965f-5ac3-88b2-f5c36bcbb4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066931} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e2bdb5-28c1-5e47-9d71-63e88a5fa7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066932} {:answer/id #uuid "594c783e-3765-5127-90ab-1555565cfd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066933} {:answer/id #uuid "61e2cd6e-d72f-5c5d-807c-39fcd802585c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594c783e-3765-5127-90ab-1555565cfd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066933} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d02f6db-f390-53f2-926e-7229d57f1fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066929}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b82792e4-e045-5093-b138-2f1c0f9db676" :question/math-academy-id "q-38328" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 2$ and $lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) =-1$, then what is $lim_(x → 2^{+})(f(x) + f(2))$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=f(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(f(x)+f(2)) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)+f(2) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124a9328-b83b-527a-a5ab-f1aa92890b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251775} {:answer/id #uuid "3afb8365-ed02-5316-b45a-395ca92a0387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251776} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6e9156-4ccf-53c2-bb5c-a1e91ccb29cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251777} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6329c6-37d6-5df0-bab7-63762383b3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251778} {:answer/id #uuid "35e513e2-4478-554b-87f5-f313666b3fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124a9328-b83b-527a-a5ab-f1aa92890b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251775} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cc2f62-69a0-5c27-8c1b-a68a79654228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a73a0e01-eeb1-509f-a99b-e23afee7b7c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38546" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/8fdb835ed2998fae4d8afc3fd5e593bac6c72b1ee45c8b4fe01bdc4348322d1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ba3cee-beb5-5de3-b100-b4659399a29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066942} {:answer/id #uuid "3470f400-ebd9-5d32-b18a-976db2abfe55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066943} {:answer/id #uuid "35964aec-3f92-51fb-aa69-3ca00fc71286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066944} {:answer/id #uuid "439442ad-0a59-5a25-8fdf-5fc9f989cad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066945} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5cc722-d385-5744-acb7-7fa6ba2176de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ba3cee-beb5-5de3-b100-b4659399a29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066942} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91092ed-69cb-59c8-ab12-a23d55bdda8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff6510c0-b13d-5580-9ffe-72088a4d30a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-38548" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/q-38548.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y = f(x)$ above?\n\n1. The function is left-continuous at $x =-1$\n2. The function is right-continuous at $x =-1$\n3. The function is continuous at $x =-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b5a7d7-de99-591c-8464-5001d3f6eada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/e48b7c05780cd477610fd0fc885fdfec406d7e557da2b02c9216557eb8e87af3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251782} {:answer/id #uuid "6edd30ba-9739-53e6-b243-b3d0d8407296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/f3ea484b37d5fba6a535189ad398ca728c79a70cdab5d8aaeb08df89be2da110.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251783} {:answer/id #uuid "49a7dccd-efd4-501f-ad08-c44dc639f75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/2153e8505d5962771a763be01e8fc3337f34516095a354b483e52d1171e0ee3c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251784} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1844b7-b655-5430-b753-bc010280f31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/b0c5521372b10882b6f5357cb678aef1274df935e7626bec930e3e084674ba62.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251785} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad2f3c7-5fbc-5544-b7c6-5f583145cf35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/a8d239b7177df7deeebb363661bab5384ded495ae8cb0ccb7ec8e55531b9eb31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd1844b7-b655-5430-b753-bc010280f31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/b0c5521372b10882b6f5357cb678aef1274df935e7626bec930e3e084674ba62.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251785} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2c99e40-08fc-5dfb-ae5f-deb5c7523c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abc90c9d-8ddb-5da3-8527-777012b6973c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48272" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not continuous at $x=2$ but is right-continuous at that point?" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function plotted below has a discontinuity at $x=2,$ but is right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/b0c5521372b10882b6f5357cb678aef1274df935e7626bec930e3e084674ba62.png)\n\nIndeed, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(2).\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f72829e6-9b0c-5ffb-af82-22f6fe043c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251789} {:answer/id #uuid "86f28d33-c9f1-586f-bb7e-be126639db77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251790} {:answer/id #uuid "990b81df-e422-5c50-94fa-05c0ae079607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251791} {:answer/id #uuid "d533677d-2133-54c8-b120-817beeccc476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251792} {:answer/id #uuid "2f897499-a605-584e-8b8b-0ed17601335c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f72829e6-9b0c-5ffb-af82-22f6fe043c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251789} :answer-field/id #uuid "99efa492-d17b-5f74-8def-5881e270cf91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251788}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23d077ae-b48b-5fdf-8116-b40f9d5a2533" :question/math-academy-id "q-73054" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/a025dc971bd0bb473e6b47f90b68ee8e8573a7acc8e5e879eab0173be1d41dcb.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above left-continuous but is not continuous?" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-1$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne -1=f(-1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea536204-d1f3-5d2b-b1ea-0e52e7e856c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251796} {:answer/id #uuid "0d86bdee-d069-5f1e-9098-b1f4fa1975c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251797} {:answer/id #uuid "93d0a04d-24bc-59a6-829b-e34bfea4d50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251798} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1c32d4-7fbe-5838-9f88-bb4151e64257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251799} {:answer/id #uuid "ba753090-f44a-56a4-b90c-f2bd89e16ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba753090-f44a-56a4-b90c-f2bd89e16ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251800} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a5fa4e-a100-5e3b-beb4-6c70178938b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "260cf2f8-813c-5d74-b688-b2ee61ca37fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73105" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945035/assets/ad3fb8a0cce2a6dac706d7876aca0a2339fe49c05cb7aa6ae54de86735ceafab.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=3$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=3$\n- The function is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=3,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=3,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}]}
